                <pb facs="0704" n="586" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg586"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">312.1722 Sandino/20: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Morrow</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-05-04T10:00:00-06:00">May 4, 1929—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2:05 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>236. Department’s 355, May 3, 4 p.m.<anchor n="28" xml:id="d664-f1"/> The Foreign
                    Office informs me that Sandino is still in Nicaragua pending the final
                    arrangement of details concerning his transportation to Mexico. It is hoped that
                    arrangements can be made for him to sail from Puerto Cortes to Progreso and
                    thence go direct to Merida where he is to remain. It may be possible for him to
                    start within a few days. The hope was expressed at the Foreign Office that he
                    would not now be interfered with as he has definitely decided to come to Mexico
                    and has accepted the conditions laid down by the Mexican Government. The Foreign
                    Office promises to inform me of further developments.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Managua.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Morrow</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
